  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Late-inning rally recaptured a lead they nearly threw away.",
    "what_this_means": "Jazz Chisholm Jr.'s ninth-inning home run flipped a one-run deficit into a 5\u20133 win, but the real problem lived in the sixth inning. They had runners on in scoring position with a chance to build a cushion. They did nothing. Washington tied it in the seventh. The game could have been over by the fifth. Instead it came down to one swing in the ninth because the offense wasted its early chance to put it away. Ryan Weathers gave them five and a third of clean baseball. One run, six strikeouts. That should have been enough. It almost wasn't. David Bednar closed the door in the ninth with two scoreless innings, clean. But a one-run win when you left six on base is not a formula that repeats.",
    "what_to_watch": "Miles Mikolas takes the mound tonight with a 5.78 ERA. He won't need much.",
